    "hpLowF": 40,
    "hpHighF": 90,
    "local": {
      "heading": "What makes a North Carolina replacement different",
      "paras": [
        "North Carolina is one of the few states we cover where the federal appliance rebate is open: Energy Saver North Carolina pays up to $1,750 toward a heat pump water heater for income-qualified homes, installed by a program-registered contractor.",
        "Most homes here heat water with electricity (75%), and state law lets a licensed plumber swap like for like without a permit. Incoming water averages 65.5 °F and drops to about 55.2 °F in February, mild enough that a heat pump in a garage holds most of its savings, about $304 a year against a standard electric tank."
      ]
    },
    "highlights": [
      "Electricity is cheaper than average in North Carolina (14.00 cents per kWh, 19% below the U.S. average), so a standard electric tank costs about $403 a year to run here, against $226 for gas.",
      "75% of North Carolina homes heat water with electricity, against 46% nationally, so most replacements here are electric-to-electric and need no gas or vent work.",
      "Of the 10 states we cover, North Carolina is one where the state's official page confirms the federal appliance rebate pays for a heat pump water heater now: up to $1,750 for income-qualified homes.",
      "Where you live inside North Carolina matters: the same 50-gallon gas job is about $1,792 in the Raleigh-Cary, NC area and $1,736 in the Winston-Salem, NC area.",
      "North Carolina law can waive the permit for a like-for-like swap."
    ]
  },
